# Fresher Career Insights – Data Analysis Project

## Project Overview
This project analyses student and fresher career data to understand placement outcomes, job preferences, skills, training needs, salary expectations, and internship experience.

The project uses Python for data analysis and visualization to identify patterns and insights that can support career-readiness planning.

## Objectives
- Analyse student placement outcomes.
- Compare placement rates across degree streams.
- Understand preferred job sectors, roles, and locations.
- Explore salary expectations.
- Identify common student skills.
- Analyse training requirements and potential skill gaps.
- Understand the relationship between internship experience and placement outcomes.

## Tools & Technologies
- Python
- Pandas
- Matplotlib
- VS Code
- CSV Dataset

## Project Analysis
This project contains 46 Python analysis steps, including:

- Overall placement KPI analysis
- Placement status analysis
- Placement by degree stream and degree level
- Placement by CGPA and experience
- Placement by graduation year and age group
- Placement by preferred sector and job role
- Placement by work mode
- Internship experience analysis
- Salary expectation analysis
- Student skill analysis
- Top skills among placed students and job seekers
- Training requirement analysis
- Skill gap analysis
- Final career insights dashboard

## Key Findings

- Total students analysed: 1,000
- Students placed: 90
- Overall placement rate: 9.00%
- Management placement rate: 11.24%
- Engineering placement rate: 10.65%
- Private sector placement rate: 9.54%
- Highest placement rate among preferred job roles: MIS Executive – 12.00%

## Final Dashboard
The final dashboard summarizes:
- Overall placement rate
- Placement by degree stream
- Placement by preferred sector
- Top job roles by placement rate

## Project Structure
- Dataset – Raw and cleaned datasets
- Python – Python analysis scripts
- Output – Generated charts and dashboard

## Conclusion
This project demonstrates data analysis skills using Python, including data grouping, aggregation, percentage calculations, comparison of categories, and visualization.

**Dataset note:** Findings are descriptive and depend on the dataset used. They should not be interpreted as causal relationships or generalized to all students or the job market.
